title: M365 Token Spray and Replay
page: breaches/m365-token-spray-replay.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: Huntress says an operator replayed stolen Microsoft 365 sessions, minted after MFA, from one rented /24, then registered rogue Entra devices, swapped MFA methods and hid mail.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 79 | organizations | where Huntress responded to related incidents
stat: 497+ | successful sign-ins | against 200,000+ failed attempts traced to the same /24
stat: 48+ | rogue Entra devices | registered across the campaign
stat: 19 | organizations | with MFA wiped and replaced within three hours of the first campaign sign-in
All four are Huntress's figures, from its own customer environments. Huntress names the replay range's hosting provider, 1337 Services GmbH, and no actor.
notes:
Start with the four figures the page leads with, and with who measured them. All four come from Huntress, which published its account on October 22, 2026, and they come from its own customer environments.

Huntress responded to related incidents at 79 organizations. It detected at least 497 successful Microsoft 365 sign-in events, against more than 200,000 failed login attempts it traced to the same address block. It counts at least 48 rogue Entra devices registered across the campaign. In 19 organizations, the victim's multi-factor authentication (MFA) methods were wiped and replaced within three hours of the first campaign sign-in.

The campaign is unattributed. Huntress names 1337 Services GmbH, the hosting provider of the address block the replays came from, and does not name an actor.

Two things you may have read elsewhere are not on this slide. The 97 compromised accounts in The Hacker News's headline come from a graphic in Huntress's report, and Huntress's text never repeats that figure. And Huntress reports no confirmed financial loss or data theft. Both come back in a few slides.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: July 14, 2026 | Huntress first detects the campaign
event: July 21 | The one case Huntress gives in full
event: By August 3 | 37 organizations signed in to, 37 rogue Entra devices registered
event: September 11 | The busiest burst, from 193.26.115[.]72
event: September 22 | A captured session replayed four minutes later
event: October 22 | Huntress publishes its account
Huntress tracked the operator from July 14 to September 22. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

Huntress first detected the campaign on July 14, 2026, as a sign-in from 193.26.115[.]221 by an Entra-joined device with a name of the form stage 04 describes. On July 21 comes the one case Huntress gives in full, on the next slide. By August 3 the operator had signed in to 37 organizations and registered 37 rogue Entra devices.

The busiest burst came from 193.26.115[.]72 on September 11, and within two hours Huntress had sent incident reports to 22 customers. On September 22 a victim completed MFA through a later relay, and the page gives four minutes from capture to replay that day. That date closes the window Huntress tracked.

Huntress published its account on October 22. Across the window the operator rotated through six addresses in one rented block over four phases, which stage 03 covers.

Entra is Microsoft Entra ID, Microsoft's cloud identity service.

== July 21: the one case Huntress gives in full
layout: timeline
event: 17:18:20 UTC | An adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) relay at 185.238.2[.]195 catches the victim at the MFA prompt
event: 17:30:21 | The session is hijacked from 193.26.115[.]199
event: 17:43:15 | The same session is in use from 124.198.132[.]33, also 1337 Services
event: 18:08:50 | A new authenticator app labelled iPhone 13 is activated
The replay came 12 minutes after the capture, and the new authenticator about 38 minutes after the hijack. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Huntress gives one case in full, and its timestamps let you follow a single session. All times are UTC.

At 17:18:20 an adversary-in-the-middle relay at 185.238.2[.]195 caught the victim at the MFA prompt. AiTM means a relay sits between the victim and the real sign-in page. This one passed the prompt through to Microsoft and kept the session that resulted. That session was issued after MFA succeeded, so replaying it did not trigger MFA again.

At 17:30:21, twelve minutes after the capture, the session was hijacked from 193.26.115[.]199. At 17:43:15 the same session was in use from 124.198.132[.]33, a second 1337 Services address.

At 18:08:50 a new authenticator app labelled iPhone 13 was activated. That is about 38 minutes after the hijack and about 50 minutes after the relay caught the victim. The Hacker News reported less than 51 minutes after the hijack, which matches Huntress's timestamps only when measured from the relay capture.

The page does not say whether this account also got a rogue device or inbox rules.

== Three caveats the page puts beside the headline
layout: compare
col: What is counted | Huntress's text counts organizations and sign-in events | The Hacker News's 97 compromised accounts come from a Huntress graphic | The text never repeats 97 or says how accounts were counted
col: Token sources | Several, per Huntress | AiTM relays, device-code phishing, social engineering during a real login | No breakdown of how many tokens came from each
col: What was lost | No confirmed financial loss or data theft reported | Huntress's figures come from its own customer environments
notes:
The page puts three caveats next to its headline, and they belong in any retelling.

First, what is counted. Huntress's text counts organizations and sign-in events. The 97 compromised accounts in The Hacker News's headline come from a "by the numbers" graphic in Huntress's report. The text never repeats that figure or says how the accounts were counted, so when you cite 97, say it comes from the graphic.

Second, where the tokens came from. Huntress says they came from several sources, including adversary-in-the-middle relays and device-code phishing, and stage 01 adds social engineering during a legitimate login. It gives no breakdown of how many tokens came from each.

Third, what was lost. Huntress reports no confirmed financial loss or data theft, and its figures come from its own customer environments.

== Figures to cite with care
layout: table
row: Figure | Source | What the page says
row: 97 compromised accounts | The Hacker News, from a Huntress graphic | Huntress's text never repeats it or explains the count
row: 193.26.115[.]72: 303 sign-ins, 29 organizations | Huntress's Phase 3 section | Its mitigation section says 232 across 28; not reconciled
row: Under 51 minutes after the hijack | The Hacker News, on the MFA swap | Matches Huntress only when measured from the relay capture
row: About 90 minutes | Huntress, on the access token after a reset | Microsoft's revoke page: one hour; token docs: 60 to 90 minutes
notes:
Four figures need care when you cite them, and the page keeps every version of each.

The Hacker News headline says 97 compromised accounts. That number comes from a graphic in Huntress's report, and the report's text never repeats it or says how the accounts were counted.

For the busiest address, 193.26.115[.]72, Huntress's Phase 3 section gives 303 successful sign-ins across 29 organizations over four days. Its mitigation section gives 232 across 28. The report does not reconcile the two.

The Hacker News reported the MFA swap in the July 21 case as less than 51 minutes after the hijack. Huntress's timestamps put the new authenticator about 38 minutes after the hijack and about 50 minutes after the relay capture, so the 51-minute figure fits only when measured from the capture.

Huntress says the access token stays valid for about 90 minutes after a password reset. Microsoft's emergency revocation page gives one hour as the default access token lifetime, and Microsoft's token-lifetime documentation says 60 to 90 minutes. The defenses come back to this.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Fake sign-in pages and device codes | T1566.002
step: 02 | Credential Access | A session minted after MFA | T1557
step: 03 | Lateral Movement | Replayed from one rented range | T1550.001
step: 04 | Persistence | Rogue Entra devices | T1098.005
step: 05 | Persistence | The victim's MFA replaced | T1556.006
step: 06 | Stealth | Inbox rules with forgettable names | T1564.008
Arrows show stage order across the campaign; the scope of each stage differs.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is where the tokens came from: fake sign-in pages behind adversary-in-the-middle relays, device-code phishing, and social engineering. Stage 02 is the capture, a relay keeping a session issued after MFA. Stage 03 is the replay from one rented address block, which Huntress calls token spraying. Stages 04 and 05 are both persistence: rogue Entra devices, and the victim's MFA methods wiped and replaced. Stage 06, under Stealth, is inbox rules that hid mail.

Read the arrows as stage order across the campaign. The scopes differ by stage: Huntress responded at 79 organizations, counts at least 48 rogue devices, and saw MFA wiped and replaced within three hours of the first campaign sign-in in 19 organizations. The July 21 case shows stages 02, 03 and 05 for one account. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Huntress names more than one source for the stolen tokens
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.002 | Phishing: Spearphishing Link
fact: Sources | AiTM, device code, social engineering; no breakdown by method
fact: Tied to a source | AiTM: 193.26.115[.]199 and [.]210; device code: 193.26.115[.]16
fact: Not described | how lures reached victims; the scripted replays' token source
In device-code phishing the victim is tricked into entering a code on Microsoft's own sign-in page; completing it hands the attacker valid tokens.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the stolen tokens came from. The page maps it to T1566.002, Phishing: Spearphishing Link.

Huntress says the tokens came from multiple sources: adversary-in-the-middle relays behind fake sign-in pages, device-code phishing, and social engineering during a legitimate login. It lists these for the campaign as a whole and gives no breakdown by method.

It does tie some replays to a source. The browser sessions replayed from 193.26.115[.]199 and 193.26.115[.]210 came through AiTM relays. In the second phase Huntress recorded sign-ins through the Microsoft Office device-code flow from 193.26.115[.]16. In that flow the victim is tricked into entering a code on Microsoft's own sign-in page, and completing it hands the attacker a valid refresh token and access token.

Keep two gaps in view. Huntress does not say where the scripted replays got their tokens, and it does not describe how the lures reached victims.

The next slide explains, in general, what the device-code flow is for.

== What the device-code sign-in flow is for
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, OAuth 2.0 device authorization grant | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-oauth2-device-code
The device authorization grant lets people sign in to input-constrained devices, such as a smart TV or printer. The user signs in from a browser on another device, entering a short code. The device then gets access and refresh tokens.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from Microsoft Learn and says nothing about the incident.

Microsoft's identity platform supports the OAuth 2.0 device authorization grant. It lets users sign in to input-constrained devices, such as a smart TV, an IoT device or a printer.

The device first asks the sign-in service for a device code and a short user code. It shows the user that code and a web address. The user visits the address in a browser on another device, such as a phone or a PC, enters the code and signs in. Meanwhile the device keeps polling the token endpoint. Once the user has signed in, the device can get access tokens and refresh tokens as needed.

== July 21: a relay caught a victim at the MFA prompt
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1557 | Adversary-in-the-Middle
fact: July 21 relay | 185.238.2[.]195 caught the victim at 17:18:20 UTC
fact: Two tiers | capture relays and replay hosts on different providers, per Huntress
fact: Later relay | an IPv6 address at Evoxt Sdn. Bhd., first seen September 2
The session token is issued after MFA succeeds, so replaying it does not trigger MFA again.
notes:
Stage 02 is the capture. The page maps it to T1557, Adversary-in-the-Middle.

In the July 21 case, at 17:18:20 UTC, an AiTM relay at 185.238.2[.]195 caught the victim at the MFA prompt. The relay passed the prompt through to Microsoft and kept the resulting session. The session token is issued after MFA succeeds, so replaying it does not trigger MFA again. The page's summary puts it this way: because each session was minted after the victim passed MFA, replaying it needed no second factor.

Huntress describes the operation as two tiers on different providers: relays that capture sessions, and separate hosts that replay them. Stage 03 is the replay tier.

A later relay, an IPv6 address at Evoxt Sdn. Bhd., first appeared on September 2. On September 22 a victim reached the MFA prompt through it at 20:46 UTC and completed MFA a minute later.

The page ties no defense to this stage, and the discussion at the end comes back to that.

== The stolen sessions were replayed from one rented /24 within minutes
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
fact: Replay range | 193.26.115.0/24 at 1337 Services GmbH, AS210558
fact: Capture to replay | 12 minutes on July 21; 4 minutes on September 22
fact: Rotation | six addresses over four phases; Entra ID Protection flagged four, one at a time
Huntress calls it token spraying: password spraying with stolen tokens, not guessed passwords. It notes a block written for one address did nothing against the next.
notes:
Stage 03 is the replay. The page maps it to T1550.001, Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token.

On July 21 the session was hijacked from 193.26.115[.]199 twelve minutes after capture. On September 22 the gap was four minutes. Across the campaign the operator rotated through six addresses in 193.26.115.0/24, a single block of addresses at 1337 Services GmbH, over four phases. AS210558 is the provider's autonomous system number, its identifier on the internet's routing system.

Microsoft Entra ID Protection independently raised verified threat actor IP detections against four of the six addresses, but flagged them one at a time, not as a range. Huntress cannot say for certain why the operator rotated addresses. It notes the effect: a block written for one address did nothing against the next, and blocking any single address would have stopped one phase at most.

Huntress calls the pattern token spraying, meaning password spraying with stolen tokens in place of guessed passwords. It traced more than 200,000 failed login attempts in the campaign window to the same range, against at least 497 successful sign-in events.

== Two builds of the replay, as Huntress describes them
layout: compare
col: Build A, scripted | A node user agent; a bare Mozilla/5.0 on one host | OAuth2:Token requests with Office and Auth Broker client IDs | Against Microsoft Graph, Exchange Online, SharePoint Online and Device Registration Service
col: Build B, browser sessions | A current Chrome or Edge user agent | Login:reprocess through the OfficeHome client | The page ties these replays to AiTM relays
Huntress does not say where Build A's tokens came from.
notes:
Huntress saw two builds of the replay.

Build A was scripted. It used a node user agent, or a bare Mozilla/5.0 on one host, and made OAuth2:Token requests with the Microsoft Office and Microsoft Authentication Broker client IDs. It made them against Microsoft Graph, Exchange Online, SharePoint Online and the Device Registration Service. A client ID identifies the application asking for a token. Huntress does not say where Build A's tokens came from.

Build B replayed browser sessions with a current Chrome or Edge user agent, through the OfficeHome client, and the page lists Login:reprocess for it. The browser sessions replayed from 193.26.115[.]199 and 193.26.115[.]210 came through AiTM relays, and the page ties AiTM to the Build B replays.

The full client IDs are on the page. The hunting slide later in the deck uses Build A's user agents and clients.

== What access tokens and refresh tokens are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Access tokens in the Microsoft identity platform | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/access-tokens
credit: Also from Refresh tokens in the Microsoft identity platform (learn.microsoft.com)
An access token grants access to specific resources on behalf of an authenticated user, and Microsoft calls access tokens sensitive credentials. A refresh token comes with it and gets a new pair of tokens when the access token expires.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft describes access tokens as security tokens designed for authorization: they grant access to specific resources on behalf of an authenticated user. The pieces of information inside a token, called claims, determine whether a user has the right to access a particular resource, much like keys that unlock specific doors in a building. That is why Microsoft calls access tokens sensitive credentials that pose a security risk if not handled correctly. An access token differs from an ID token, which serves as proof of authentication.

When a client acquires an access token, it also receives a refresh token. The refresh token is used to obtain a new access token and refresh token pair when the current access token expires. Refresh tokens have a longer lifetime than access tokens.

Also from Refresh tokens in the Microsoft identity platform: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/refresh-tokens

== The scripted build registered at least 48 rogue Entra devices
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Persistence
technique: T1098.005 | Account Manipulation: Device Registration
fact: DESKTOP- names | 8 uppercase hex characters; rare, not unique to this campaign
fact: First detection | July 14, 2026: a device so named signed in from 193.26.115[.]221
fact: Count | 37 registered by August 3; at least 48 across the campaign
A registered device can give the attacker a more durable way to request tokens than the stolen session, Huntress says, and it stays until someone deletes it.
notes:
Stage 04 is the first form of persistence. The page maps it to T1098.005, Account Manipulation: Device Registration.

Huntress first detected the campaign on July 14, 2026, as a sign-in from 193.26.115[.]221 by an Entra-joined device with a name of this form. A fake device appeared in 13 of the 14 incidents Huntress responded to that day. By August 3 the operator had signed in to 37 organizations and registered 37 rogue Entra devices, and Huntress counts at least 48 distinct devices across the campaign.

Huntress explains why the device matters. A registered device can give the attacker a more durable way to request tokens than the stolen session, and it stays in the directory until someone deletes it.

The name format, DESKTOP- plus eight uppercase hexadecimal characters, such as DESKTOP-54521F46, does not match the default Windows gives a new machine. Huntress also says the pattern is rare but not unique to this campaign, so confirm a device before deleting it.

The report gives no timing between first sign-in and device registration.

== In 19 organizations, MFA methods were wiped and a new one registered
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Persistence
technique: T1556.006 | Modify Authentication Process: Multi-Factor Authentication
fact: Timing | within three hours of the first campaign sign-in
fact: Removed through | Microsoft's security-info registration service
fact: July 21 case | an iPhone 13 authenticator app activated at 18:08:50 UTC
In Huntress's words, "A password reset alone does not remove attacker-registered devices or MFA methods."
notes:
Stage 05 is the second form of persistence. The page maps it to T1556.006, Modify Authentication Process: Multi-Factor Authentication.

In 19 organizations the victim's existing MFA methods were wiped through Microsoft's security-info registration service, and a new authenticator was registered minutes later, all within three hours of the first campaign sign-in. In the July 21 case a new authenticator app labelled iPhone 13 was activated at 18:08:50 UTC, about 38 minutes after the hijack and about 50 minutes after the relay caught the victim. In one incident the operator used a deviceless authenticator method that was already on the account.

Resetting the password does not undo any of this. Quote Huntress here: "A password reset alone does not remove attacker-registered devices or MFA methods."

Microsoft described a similar swap in Storm-2949, which has its own kill chain on csoh.org. There, existing methods were removed and an attacker-controlled Microsoft Authenticator enrolled. Microsoft assessed with high confidence that the way in was abuse of self-service password reset, with the user talked into approving MFA prompts. The page calls the swap similar and draws no other link between the two.

The next slide explains what security info is, in general.

== What security info is in Microsoft Entra
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Combined security information registration overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/concept-registration-mfa-sspr-combined
Security info is where Microsoft Entra users register the authentication methods used for both multifactor authentication and self-service password reset. From it, users can add methods, delete or change existing ones, and change their default method.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Before combined registration, users registered authentication methods for Microsoft Entra multifactor authentication and for self-service password reset, or SSPR, separately. With combined registration they register once, and the methods serve both.

The methods it supports include the Microsoft Authenticator app, other authenticator apps, phone numbers, email and passkeys. A user can make one of them the default method for multifactor authentication, such as an Authenticator push notification, a code from an authenticator app, a phone call or a text message.

Users reach it in two ways. Interrupt mode is a wizard shown at sign-in when a user must register or refresh their security info. Manage mode is the Security info page in the user's profile, where they can add methods, delete or change existing ones, and change the default.

== Inbox rules with forgettable names hid mail, one keyed to payroll
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Stealth
technique: T1564.008 | Hide Artifacts: Email Hiding Rules
fact: Rule names | ".", "sss", "ss", "iT", ",.", "........", "s" and "d"
fact: Rule s | hid messages containing payroll, direct deposit, bank or bonus
fact: September 11 | a compromised mailbox sent an outside accountant (CPA) a payroll-related email; outcome not reported
Huntress says access of this kind sets the stage for business email compromise; it does not report a completed fraud, financial loss or data theft.
notes:
Stage 06 is the cover-up. The page maps it to T1564.008, Hide Artifacts: Email Hiding Rules, under the Stealth phase.

The rules were named with a single dot, sss, ss, iT, a comma and a dot, a row of dots, s and d. Most marked mail as read and deleted it, or moved it to RSS Feeds, RSS Subscriptions or Conversation History. Rule s hid messages containing payroll, direct deposit, bank or bonus. Rule d moved all mail from one external CPA's address to Conversation History and marked it read. A CPA is a certified public accountant. In four mailboxes the rules were created through the Outlook REST API rather than Outlook itself. Every rule came from a VPN or rented hosting, and none from the 1337 Services network.

In the September 11 burst, the operator used a compromised mailbox to send a payroll-related email to an external CPA. In that mailbox it modified rule s and, five minutes later, created rule d, both from one Interserver address, 167.148.41[.]96. Huntress reports no outcome for that email.

In the first phase, a mailbox carrying the sss rule sent spam to hundreds of external recipients before Microsoft Entra ID Protection rated the account high risk.

== What business email compromise is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, BEC | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-bec
The csoh.org glossary defines business email compromise (BEC) as taking over or convincingly imitating a real mailbox to redirect money or data. Its payload is a plausible business request, not a link. Huntress does not report a completed fraud.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general term from the csoh.org glossary.

The glossary defines business email compromise as taking over or convincingly imitating a real mailbox to redirect money or data. It sets BEC apart from ordinary phishing: the payload is a plausible business request rather than a link, which, the glossary says, is why technical controls alone catch so few of them.

Keep the page's limit with the definition. Huntress says access of the kind this campaign gained sets the stage for business email compromise. It does not report a completed fraud, a financial loss or data theft.

== Six defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Revoke and reset, then remove what was registered | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Audit Entra devices and MFA methods on replayed accounts | 04 | 05
row: Conditional Access: MFA or block for all users, cloud apps and client types | 03
row: Triage by credential validity, not spray volume | 03
row: Restrict device-code flow and user consent | 01
row: Hunt the replay and the cover-up together | 03 | 06
No defense on the page is tied to stage 02, the relay capture.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its six stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something from that stage alone as what it closes or catches.

Revoking sessions and resetting the password answers the replay, stage 03: replays of sessions that were no longer valid failed with AADSTS50133. Removing what the attacker registered answers the rogue devices of stage 04 and the attacker's MFA methods of stage 05. The device and MFA audit answers the same two stages.

Conditional Access answers stage 03 through the busiest address, 193.26.115[.]72: all 1,052 sign-ins blocked from it came from four organizations, and in the 29 it got into, nothing stopped it. Triage by credential validity answers stage 03's spray. Restricting device-code flow answers the device-code phishing of stage 01. The hunts answer the scripted replay of stage 03 and the inbox rules of stage 06. The consent advice and the hunt for new enterprise applications name no stage.

Now look at the empty column. No defense on the page is tied to stage 02, the relay that caught victims at the MFA prompt. The page notes that blocking the /24 would not have covered the relays. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Revoke and reset, then remove what the attacker registered
layout: compare
col: Huntress's containment order | Revoke sessions and reset the password | Then remove rogue devices and attacker MFA methods | A reset alone leaves the access token valid about 90 minutes, per Huntress
col: Microsoft's emergency guidance | Disable the account, revoke sessions, then disable registered devices | One hour default access token lifetime | No mention of MFA methods; extra steps for hybrid tenants
Replays of sessions no longer valid failed with AADSTS50133, which Microsoft returns after a password expires or is changed: three attempts at one organization in August, one on September 3.
notes:
Huntress and Microsoft both give a containment order, and they differ.

Huntress's order is to revoke sessions and reset the password, then remove what the attacker registered. A password reset by itself does not invalidate the access token, which Huntress says stays valid for about 90 minutes, and it leaves rogue devices and attacker MFA methods in place.

Microsoft's emergency revocation guidance differs in order and scope: disable the account, revoke sessions, then disable the user's registered devices. Its page gives one hour as the default access token lifetime, while Microsoft's token-lifetime documentation says 60 to 90 minutes. It notes that an app holding an access token keeps access until that token expires, unless the service supports continuous access evaluation, which lets a service respond to events such as revoked sessions before a token expires. It does not mention MFA methods, which Huntress's list adds. In hybrid tenants, which run on-premises Active Directory alongside Entra ID, it also disables the on-premises account and resets the password twice.

The page also records replays that failed. Replays of sessions that were no longer valid failed with AADSTS50133, SsoArtifactRevoked, which Microsoft returns after a password expires or is changed: three attempts against one organization in August, and one on September 3.

== Audit devices and MFA methods on every replayed account
layout: text
- DESKTOP-, Device- or Work Pc plus eight hex characters, especially from hosting addresses
- Confirm each first: the pattern is not unique to this campaign
- MFA methods cleared and re-registered within minutes
- Unrecognized authenticators; deviceless methods already on the account
notes:
The second defense is an audit of every account that was replayed, covering both devices and authentication methods.

For devices, Huntress's audit list covers names that start with DESKTOP-, Device- or Work Pc followed by eight hexadecimal characters, particularly devices registered from hosting IP addresses. Confirm each one before deleting it, since the pattern is not unique to this campaign.

For MFA, look for methods that were cleared and re-registered within minutes, remove any authenticator the user does not recognize, and check deviceless methods already on the account, which one incident here used.

The page gives the reason for the device audit: a device left in the directory can still give the attacker a way to request tokens after the stolen session is dead.

== Require MFA or block for every user, cloud app and client type
layout: compare
col: Four organizations | All 1,052 blocked sign-ins from 193.26.115[.]72 came from them | The most sprayed, the least compromised | Which policies they had: not stated
col: 29 organizations it got into | Nothing stopped the same address there | The mitigation section says 28
Triage by credential validity, not spray volume: a Conditional Access block (AADSTS53003) comes after authentication succeeds, so Huntress treats it as evidence of a valid stolen credential.
notes:
Two of the page's defenses draw on one address, 193.26.115[.]72, the busiest in the campaign.

Huntress's main recommendation is to require MFA or block for all users, all cloud apps and all client app types in Conditional Access. Conditional Access is the Entra ID policy engine that grants access only when conditions such as device, location or risk are met. All 1,052 sign-ins blocked from that address came from four organizations, while in the 29 organizations the address got into, nothing stopped it. Huntress does not say which policies those four organizations had, and the report's mitigation section gives 28 rather than 29.

The second defense is about triage. A Conditional Access block, error AADSTS53003, happens after authentication succeeds, so Huntress treats it as evidence of a valid stolen credential rather than a defense that held. Ranking tenants by failed attempts would have pointed responders at the four where Conditional Access was already working, the most-sprayed and least-compromised ones. Triage by credential validity, not by spray volume.

== Restrict device-code flow and user consent
layout: compare
col: Huntress recommends | Conditional Access authentication-flow policies that restrict device code | Restricted service accounts and exclusions where the flow is needed | Turning off standard users' consent to new applications
col: ANY.RUN, separately | Describes a device-code phishing kit it calls SkyW4lker | The victim enters a code on Microsoft's legitimate page | Neither ANY.RUN nor Huntress connects it to this campaign
ANY.RUN gives the same Conditional Access advice.
notes:
This defense answers stage 01's device-code route.

Huntress recommends Conditional Access authentication-flow policies to restrict device code, with restricted service accounts and exclusions where the flow is needed. It also recommends turning off standard users' ability to consent to new applications. Consent here means a user granting an application access on their behalf.

Separately from this campaign, ANY.RUN has described a device-code phishing kit it calls SkyW4lker. In it, the victim enters a code on Microsoft's legitimate page and the operator's backend collects the resulting tokens. Neither ANY.RUN nor Huntress connects the two, so present SkyW4lker as an example of the technique, not as part of this campaign. ANY.RUN gives the same Conditional Access advice.

Recall from stage 01 that Huntress tied device-code sign-ins to 193.26.115[.]16 and gives no breakdown of how many tokens came from each method.

== Hunt for the replay and the cover-up together
layout: compare
col: The replay | OAuth2:Token and Cmsi:Cmsi sign-ins from hosting providers | Office or Auth Broker client; node or bare Mozilla/5.0 user agent | Especially against mailboxes or the Device Registration Service
col: The cover-up | Inbox rules with one-to-three-character or punctuation-only names | Rules routing mail to RSS Feeds, RSS Subscriptions or Conversation History | Rules keyed to payroll or banking terms
Blocking the /24 would have covered all six main replay hosts, but not the relays, a few other 1337 Services addresses, or the VPN and hosting addresses that created the rules.
notes:
Huntress's hunts look for the replay and the cover-up together.

For the replay, hunt OAuth2:Token and Cmsi:Cmsi sign-ins from hosting providers that use the Microsoft Office or Authentication Broker client with a node or bare Mozilla/5.0 user agent, especially against mailboxes or the Device Registration Service. Build A, at stage 03, used those clients and user agents.

Huntress also hunts for new enterprise applications, which can keep access to a tenant. No stage on the page describes a new application.

For the cover-up, hunt inbox rules with one-to-three-character or punctuation-only names, rules that route mail to RSS Feeds, RSS Subscriptions or Conversation History, and rules keyed to payroll or banking terms. Those are the rules of stage 06.

Know what an address block covers. Blocking the /24 would have covered all six main replay hosts, but not the relays, a few other 1337 Services addresses, or the VPN and hosting addresses that created the rules. And the device-name pattern from stage 04 is rare but not unique to this campaign, so confirm before deleting.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: After a reset, does your runbook also revoke sessions and remove devices and methods?
check: Could you list each account's Entra devices and the addresses that registered them?
check: Would you notice MFA methods cleared and re-registered within minutes?
check: Is MFA or block required for all users, cloud apps and client app types?
check: Does your triage rank tenants by valid credentials rather than failed attempts?
check: Is device-code flow restricted, and can standard users consent to new apps?
check: Would you find an inbox rule named "." that moves mail to RSS Feeds?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your runbook stops at a password reset, or also revokes sessions and removes the devices and MFA methods an attacker registered. Ask whether you could list the Entra devices on an account and where they were registered from, and whether anything would flag MFA methods cleared and re-registered within minutes.

Ask whether Conditional Access requires MFA or block for all users, all cloud apps and all client app types. Ask how your team would rank affected tenants: by failed attempts, or by evidence of valid credentials.

Ask whether device-code flow is restricted to where it is needed, and whether standard users can consent to new applications. Finally, ask whether anything would find an inbox rule with a one-character name that routes mail to RSS Feeds or Conversation History.

== After a password reset, what does the attacker still hold?
layout: question
Your team reset a replayed account's password and stopped there.

Using what this deck has shown, list what the attacker may still hold, and what would remove each.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The scenario is the one the page warns about: a password reset with nothing after it. Ask the group to work from the stages they have seen, the replayed session at stage 03, the rogue devices at stage 04 and the replaced MFA methods at stage 05, and from the containment slide.

Ask each person to name one item and how long it would last, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== A reset alone leaves three things in place
layout: answer
Huntress's order: revoke sessions, reset, then remove the devices and methods.
- The access token, until it expires: about 90 minutes per Huntress, one hour by default per Microsoft
- Rogue Entra devices, until someone deletes them
- The attacker's MFA methods
notes:
The page answers this directly. In Huntress's words, "A password reset alone does not remove attacker-registered devices or MFA methods."

First, the access token. A password reset by itself does not invalidate it, and Huntress says it stays valid for about 90 minutes. Microsoft's emergency page gives one hour as the default, and its token-lifetime documentation says 60 to 90 minutes. An app holding an access token keeps access until it expires, unless the service supports continuous access evaluation.

Second, rogue Entra devices. A registered device can give the attacker a more durable way to request tokens than the stolen session, and it stays in the directory until someone deletes it.

Third, the attacker's MFA methods, which Microsoft's emergency guidance does not mention and Huntress's list adds.

Huntress's containment order is to revoke sessions and reset the password, then remove what the attacker registered, which takes out the devices and methods. The page does not say it ends an access token already issued; Microsoft's page says that keeps working until it expires unless the service supports continuous access evaluation.

== What would you add for stage 02?
layout: question
The page's defenses are tied to stages 01 and 03 to 06. None is tied to the relay that caught a victim at the MFA prompt.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page ties defenses to stages 01, 03, 04, 05 and 06. It ties nothing to stage 02, where adversary-in-the-middle relays caught victims at the MFA prompt and kept the sessions Microsoft issued after MFA.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 02
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details: the relay at 185.238.2[.]195, the Evoxt relay first seen September 2, and capture relays on different providers from the replay hosts.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 02, and this deck adds none of its own. The group may argue that Conditional Access or the containment steps reach this stage; the page does not say so.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports for stage 02. In the July 21 case an AiTM relay at 185.238.2[.]195 caught the victim at the MFA prompt at 17:18:20 UTC, passed the prompt through to Microsoft and kept the resulting session. Huntress describes two tiers on different providers: relays that capture, and separate hosts that replay. A later relay, an IPv6 address at Evoxt Sdn. Bhd., first appeared on September 2, and on September 22 a victim completed MFA through it.

The page also notes that blocking the /24 would not have covered the relays.

